  "title": "Canine MIMIC-2 Study: Minimally Invasive Metastasectomy in Dogs | Ethos Discovery",
  "text": "Canine MIMIC-2 Study: Minimally Invasive Metastasectomy in Dogs | Ethos Discovery\nContact Us\nAbout Us\nAbout Us\nPhilanthropic Strategy\nIRS Form 990\nOur Team\nScientific Collaboration\nEthos Veterinary Health\nEthos Veterinary Training Programs\nScientific Programs\nOur Studies\nStudy Guides\nChronic Enteropathy Study\nFICUSS Study\nHemangiosarcoma Program\nInflammatory CNS Disease Program\nInnovations in Surgery\nIntestinal Dysbiosis Program\nNovel Therapeutics Program\nSepsis Program\nFAQs\nDonate\nNews + Articles\nPress Releases\nOur Blog\nContact Us\nOur Hospital Network\nContact Us\nAbout Us\nAbout Us\nPhilanthropic Strategy\nIRS Form 990\nOur Team\nScientific Collaboration\nEthos Veterinary Health\nEthos Veterinary Training Programs\nScientific Programs\nOur Studies\nStudy Guides\nChronic Enteropathy Study\nFICUSS Study\nHemangiosarcoma Program\nInflammatory CNS Disease Program\nInnovations in Surgery\nIntestinal Dysbiosis Program\nNovel Therapeutics Program\nSepsis Program\nFAQs\nDonate\nNews + Articles\nPress Releases\nOur Blog\nContact Us\nOur Hospital Network\nContact Us\nContact Us\nSign-ups for our Discovery Walk for Dogs now open!\nLearn more here\nClinical Studies\nMinimally Invasive Metastasectomy in Canines (MIMIC)\nOpen\nCanine Osteosarcoma Treatment\nMinimally-invasive surgical bone cancer treatment for dogs\nCURRENTLY ENROLLING\nThe MIMIC clinical trial is open for enrollment. Ethos Discovery is investigating a new minimally-invasive surgical treatment for canine osteosarcoma that has metastasized, or spread, to the lungs. This study is subsidized and all procedures will be provided at an affordable, low cost.\nEligibility Criteria:\n• Dogs weighing >5 kgs with a diagnosis of osteosarcoma\n• Local tumor controlled or able to be controlled through surgery or radiation\n• Suspected or confirmed cancer that has metastasized in the lungs\nThe Objective of This Study:\nThe use of surgery to manage osteosarcoma in dogs has been shown to be promising, but is not commonly used due to the risks of conventional thoracic surgery. We seek to reduce risks and improve\noutcomes using minimally invasive surgical techniques. These trials will pave the way to conduct accelerated studies to identify new drugs to prevent lung metastases in both human and canine osteosarcoma.\nWhat to Expect:\nA surgical technique using miniature incisions in the chest wall to allow removal of lung nodules with the assistance of a magnified video camera. This has the potential to minimize the pain, morbidity, and recovery\ntime to patients at a fraction of what’s observed with conventional techniques. We predict patients will be recovered within 2 days and able to begin returning to normal life.\nParticipation in a clinical study is subject to medical eligibility and availability requirements. Financial incentives, including potential stipends or reimbursements, are contingent upon meeting study criteria and completing required visits or activities. Additional terms and conditions may apply.\nShare\nFrequently Asked Questions\nClinical Studies\nPurina Fecal Score Chart\nParticipating Hospitals\nVeterinary Specialty Hospital – North County\nGulf Coast Veterinary Specialists\nΔ\nEmail\nThis field is for validation purposes and should be left unchanged.\nName\n*\nEmail\n*\nPhone\nPet's Name\nComments\nSubmit\n100% of Your Donation Supports Our Research\nContact Us\n© 2026 Ethos Discovery\nWebsite Design\nby\nJackrabbit\n;",
